# The functions a C caller can reach that Verus does not check
# (verus/count, reach.rs; verus/README.md, "Entry points").
#
# verus/count walks every call from each entry point of a shipped crate,
# through every shipped crate, resolving calls by name. The entry points
# are:
#
# - each function that exports its symbol (no_mangle or export_name):
#   {EXPORTED_EXAMPLES};
# - each function a const or static hands out ({ADDRESSED_EXAMPLES});
# - {PORT_SPECIFIC_ROOTS: each body an exports macro writes wrappers for,
#   and the conversions those wrappers apply, as the counter's
#   configuration names them; delete this item when the port has none}.
#
# Functions and impl methods declared inside a function body are walked
# too. Each function the walk reaches is verified, discharged by Kani, or a
# row here:
#
#   FILE NAME REASON
#
# NAME is `Owner::name` for a method (its owner's base type), else `name`.
# REASON starts with the function's class (`trusted`: inside verus! but
# external_body, or minting a token on trust; `outside`: not inside
# verus!) and says why: the seam whose contract it is (SEAMS.md, or the
# seams table of the theorem that names it), or what keeps it out of
# verus!. A `trusted` row cites at least one seam, and every seam ID a row
# cites must be a row of those tables, or the run fails.
#
# The run fails on a reachable function that is neither verified,
# discharged nor listed; on a row that is verified or that no entry point
# reaches, so a change that verifies a function drops its row; and, with
# --base-rev (the verus check passes the pull request's base), on a row
# the base's REACH does not have, unless it starts with `new`:
#
#   new FILE NAME REASON
#
# So the list only shrinks unless a change says otherwise in the row
# itself: one that adds an unverified function a C caller reaches, or
# moves or renames one, marks its row `new`.
#
# kit: proved-port v{KIT_VERSION} ({KIT_SHA})
